config.py — Central Configuration & System Defaults

Purpose

config.py defines every core parameter that controls robot behavior, motion-planning settings, perception options, and communication reliability. It ensures all other modules: node.py, motions.py, perception.py, etc.—share consistent values for frames, joints, QoS, and URDF configuration.



It also allows you to override defaults from:

ROS 2 parameters (declared in node.py)

Environment variables (loaded by AppConfig.from_env())

ROS 2 Quality-of-Service (QoS) profiles

Robot model & world definitions

Default motion-planner parameters

Joint position presets (home / drop-off)

Perception (YOLO + ZED) parameters

Environment variable overrides for runtime customization


Quality of Service (QoS) Definitions

DEFAULT_QOS = QoSProfile(
    reliability=ReliabilityPolicy.RELIABLE,
    history=HistoryPolicy.KEEP_LAST,
    depth=5,
    durability=DurabilityPolicy.VOLATILE,
)

reliability=RELIABLE	Guarantees message delivery.	Essential for command topics.
depth=5	Stores last 5 messages.	Prevents buffer overflow.
durability=VOLATILE	No re-delivery to late subscribers.	Keeps real-time freshness.



New stuff:
@dataclass is a shortcut in Python.
It helps you make a class that’s just used to store data — without writing extra code.
Normally, you’d write:
class Person:
    def __init__(self, name, age):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age

Now you can do:

p = Person("Ali", 25)
print(p.name)  # Ali

With @dataclass, you just write:
from dataclasses import dataclass

@dataclass
class Person:
    name: str
    age: int
    
    __init__

This is the constructor — the function that runs when you make an object from a class.

Example: 

class Person:
    def __init__(self, name, age):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age

Now when you do:

p = Person("Ali", 25)

Python calls __init__ automatically and sets:

p.name = "Ali"
p.age = 25

If you use @dataclass, Python writes this function for you automatically — so you don’t have to.
🧩 2. __repr__

This is the string version of your object — what Python shows when you print it.

Example:

class Person:
    def __init__(self, name, age):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age
    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Person(name={self.name}, age={self.age})"

Now:

p = Person("Ali", 25)
print(p)

Output:

Person(name=Ali, age=25)

If you use @dataclass, Python automatically creates this pretty-print version for you too.
🧩 3. __eq__

This is used when you compare two objects with ==.

Example:

a = Person("Ali", 25)
b = Person("Ali", 25)
print(a == b)  # False normally

Normally, Python would say False because it thinks they are different objects in memory.

But if you use @dataclass, it automatically adds __eq__, which means:

    “Two dataclass objects are equal if all their variables have the same values.”

So now:

from dataclasses import dataclass

@dataclass
class Person:
    name: str
    age: int

a = Person("Ali", 25)
b = Person("Ali", 25)
print(a == b)  # True ✅


2) home: List[float] = field(default_factory=lambda: [0.0, -1.57, 1.57, 0.0, 1.57, 0.0])

home:	This defines a variable (or attribute) named home.
List[float]	The type of data: it’s a list of numbers (floats).
=	We’re giving it a default value.
field(...)	This is a helper from the dataclasses module that lets you control how defaults are made.
default_factory=lambda: [...]	This means: “When creating a new object, make a fresh copy of this list each time.”

2. class AppConfig:

This defines a class called AppConfig, which means “Application Configuration.”
It stores all your settings in one place.

Think of it like a big folder that contains four smaller folders:

one for topics,

one for joint presets,

one for planner settings,

one for perception settings.

3. Each line inside the class

Let’s go line by line 👇

🧩 topics: Topics = field(default_factory=Topics)

topics is the name of this setting group.

Its type is another class called Topics.

field(default_factory=Topics) means:

“When you create a new AppConfig, automatically make a new Topics object inside it.”

